      "In the U.S. Health and Retirement Study, book readers had a survival advantage over 12 years compared with non-readers, larger than for newspaper or magazine readers and partly mediated by cognition. A 14-year Taiwanese cohort found people who read at least weekly had roughly half the odds of later cognitive decline at every education level. The claim that a single passage of literary fiction improves theory of mind was supported in the original Science experiments but not in a 792-person replication; the original authors' own preregistered replications gave mixed results, though lifetime exposure to fiction consistently correlated with mind-reading ability.",
