    description:
      "Chess and video games are fine hobbies, but the cognitive-transfer claims are weak. Sala and Gobet's meta-analysis of chess instruction found small-to-moderate effects on academic and cognitive skills that were inversely related to study quality; their comprehensive meta-analysis of video-game training (hundreds of effect sizes) found small or null effects on cognitive ability and no evidence of a causal benefit. Bediou and colleagues' meta-analysis restricted to action video games found a moderate cross-sectional advantage for habitual players and a smaller intervention effect on attention and spatial cognition, with publication bias likely inflating both.",
