    feeds: ["cognitive reserve", "mood", "independence"],
    description:
      "Multiple long cohorts converge: frequent participation in cognitively stimulating activities was associated with a 33 percent lower risk of Alzheimer disease in the Religious Orders Study; daily-to-weekly mental, social or productive activities roughly halved dementia incidence in the Kungsholmen Project; and a meta-analysis of 38 longitudinal studies (over two million participants) found cognitive, physical and social leisure activities each associated with lower all-cause dementia incidence. Fixed-effects analyses of hobbies and depression in the same people over time support hobby engagement preceding lower depressive symptoms. Stern's cognitive-reserve framework explains why later-life activity can buffer brain changes. The 'use it or lose it' caveat stands: high-ability people also choose active lives, so the causal share is uncertain.",
